[{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Available-options","page":"Overview","text":"Up: pack()\n\nEvery keyword of pack(), grouped, with its default and a link to its page. A keyword page lists the choices for that keyword and says which is the default. A choice that has settings of its own, such as Bidisperse(), links on to a page of its own.","title":"Available options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Particles-and-contacts","page":"Overview","text":"Who the particles are and how they touch.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\ndimension 2D disks or 3D spheres 2 3\nparticles_count the number of particles 256 any N ≥ 2\nparticles_size_distribution how particle diameters are drawn Bidisperse() Bidisperse(), UniformPolydisperse(), Monodisperse()\nparticles_diameters an explicit list of diameters nothing a vector\ncontact the contact force law Hookean() Hookean(), Hertzian()\nfriction tangential contact forces nothing CundallStrack(), Mindlin()\nmass the particle masses EqualMass() EqualMass(), Density()\ntarget the pressure the compression runs to Pressure(1e-4) Pressure()","title":"Particles and contacts"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Box,-start-and-compression","page":"Overview","text":"The box and its boundary, the particles' starting positions, and how the box is shrunk to the target pressure.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\nboundary periodic boundaries or rigid walls Periodic() Periodic(), Walls()\ninitial_state where the compression starts Dilute(packing_fraction = 0.25) Dilute()\nprotocol how the box is compressed IsotropicCompression(strain_step = 1e-2) IsotropicCompression()","title":"Box, start and compression"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Stopping,-failure-and-restarts","page":"Overview","text":"When a run stops, what happens if it does not converge, and how to continue it.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\nconvergence when a run counts as converged RelativeForce() RelativeForce(), LeidenAcceleration(), KineticEnergy(), RestartTest(), Your own test function\npressure_tolerance how close the pressure must come to the target 1e-4 any value\nsteps_max the step cap per call 10^7 any value\nfailure_action what happens when a run does not converge :warn :error\nrestart_packing continue from a saved run nothing path to a file\nrestart_file save the run state during the run nothing RestartFile()","title":"Stopping, failure and restarts"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Algorithm,-damping-and-time-step","page":"Overview","text":"How the packing is relaxed. damping, drag and integrator are not keywords of pack(): they are keywords of the algorithm, so they sit one level down, under algorithm.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\nalgorithm the minimizer or dynamics FIRE() FIRE(), DampedDynamics(); inside them: damping, drag, integrator\ntimestep the size of the time step CollisionTime() CollisionTime(), fixed number","title":"Algorithm, damping and time step"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Rattlers","page":"Overview","text":"Which particles are rattlers and whether they are removed.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\nrattlers which particles are rattlers and whether to remove them ContactCount() ContactCount(), TwoContact(), ForceFloor(), Unresolved(), Your own rule function, nothing","title":"Rattlers"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Reproducibility-and-outputs","page":"Overview","text":"The random seed and what is recorded during the run.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\nseed the random seed 1 any integer\nsnapshots record the configuration during the run nothing Snapshots()\nmovie render a movie during the run nothing Movie()","title":"Reproducibility and outputs"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Where-is-damping?","page":"Overview","text":"note: damping, drag and integrator are keywords of the algorithm, not of `pack()`\nThe algorithm decides whether there are dashpots at all. FIRE() takes damping and drag and defaults both to nothing. DampedDynamics() takes the same two keywords and defaults damping to ContactDamping(), and it also takes integrator, which defaults to :centered_velocity_verlet.\n\npack(                                              # run the packing\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(                    # damped dynamics\n        damping    = ContactDamping(normal = 0.98),   # contact dashpots, normal ratio 0.98\n        drag       = nothing,                         # no Stokes drag\n        integrator = :centered_velocity_verlet,       # dashpot on the end-of-step velocity (default)\n    )                                              # end of DampedDynamics call\n)                                                  # end of pack call\n\ndamping: the dashpot models ContactDamping(), ViscousRate() and RelativeVelocityDamping().\ndrag: Stokes drag, StokesDrag().\nintegrator: the time stepper, :centered_velocity_verlet, :lagged_velocity_verlet or :semi_implicit_euler.\nFIRE(): the default algorithm, with no damping and no drag.","title":"Where is damping?"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Beyond-the-keywords","page":"Overview","text":"PackParams(): bundle keywords into a reusable value and call pack(params).\nPacking: what pack() returns.\nfind_rattlers(): apply a rattler rule to a finished packing.\nYour own test function and Your own rule function: any function can be a convergence test or a rattler rule.\nwrite_packing(): save a packing to disk.","title":"Beyond the keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/units/#Units-and-length-unit","page":"Units and length unit","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Units; Changing the length unit; Time unit and contact time scales.\n\nD = 1, k = 1, m = 1 (old §2–§3).","title":"Units and length unit"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/references/#References","page":"References","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nwarning: Not moved to this site yet\nThis page is still on the wiki: References.\n\nFull citations for every source.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/theory/#Theory-pages","page":"Theory pages","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nEach option page on this site says what to type, what the default is, and why. The pages below hold the models, the derivations and the worked numbers behind those defaults. They are still on the wiki, one page per section of the design document, and each option page links to the wiki section that matches it.\n\n§ Page What it covers\n1 Interface and naming pack(), keywords, PackParams, naming rules\n2 Default packing Dimension, contact law, units, pressure, frictionless default\n3 Particle size distribution and length unit Bidisperse mixture, diameter ratio, length unit\n4 Mass EqualMass and Density, effective masses\n5 Convergence criterion Force, torque, pressure and kinetic-energy tests\n6 A packing that does not converge Time limits, restarts, restart files\n7 Algorithms and combinations FIRE and damped dynamics, allowed combinations\n8 Damping Contact dashpots, Stokes drag, critical damping, Rayleigh damping, mode decay\n9 Rattler rules Which particles are rattlers and how they are removed\n10 Reproducibility Seeds and random-number generators\n11 Outputs File formats, stored fields, snapshots and movies\n12 Time step Collision time, step size, stability\n13 Friction models Cundall–Strack and Mindlin, friction coefficient, stiffness ratio\n14 FIRE settings The FIRE 2.0 algorithm and its parameters\n\nThe symbols are on the Notation page and the sources on the References page.","title":"Theory pages"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hertzian/#Hertzian()","page":"Hertzian()","text":"Up: contact\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Hertzian contacts; Contact law.","title":"Hertzian()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hertzian/#Options","page":"Hertzian()","text":"stiffness","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/notation/#Notation","page":"Notation","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nwarning: Not moved to this site yet\nThis page is still on the wiki: Notation.\n\nEvery symbol, its meaning and units.","title":"Notation"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/Density/#Density()","page":"Density()","text":"Up: mass\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Density(shape = …); Density masses.","title":"Density()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/Density/#Options","page":"Density()","text":"density\nshape","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/#convergence","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Convergence criterion.\n\nWhen to stop.\n\nEach choice decides when the packing is at rest at the current box, and pressure_tolerance is checked after every one of them. LeidenAcceleration() and your own function replace the force test of RelativeForce(), and KineticEnergy() and RestartTest() add a check to it at its defaults.\n\nDefault: RelativeForce()","title":"convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nRelativeForce() default\nLeidenAcceleration() \nKineticEnergy() \nRestartTest() \nYour own test function ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/EqualMass/#EqualMass()","page":"EqualMass()","text":"Up: mass\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: EqualMass(…); Equal masses make contact damping proportional to stiffness.\n\nThis is the default for mass.","title":"EqualMass()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/EqualMass/#Options","page":"EqualMass()","text":"mass","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/find_rattlers/#find_rattlers()","page":"find_rattlers()","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: find_rattlers.\n\nApply any rule to a finished packing.","title":"find_rattlers()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#:semi_implicit_euler","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"Up: integrator\n\nKick the velocity with the current force, then drift the positions with the new velocity, with the dashpot on the old velocity mathbf v_n. It is the integrator FIRE() uses by default, here applied to DampedDynamics(). It is the cheapest of the three and has no half-step state, but it is first order and its stable step shrinks as damping grows, the same as lagged velocity Verlet.","title":":semi_implicit_euler"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#The-call-with-every-option-written-out","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"pack(                                       # run the packing\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(             # damped dynamics instead of the default FIRE\n        integrator = :semi_implicit_euler,  # kick with the current force, then drift\n    )                                       # end of DampedDynamics call\n)                                           # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nalgorithm = DampedDynamics(...) — damped dynamics, Newton's equations with contact dashpots.\nintegrator = :semi_implicit_euler — the dashpot force uses the velocity at the start of the step mathbf v_n.\nThis is an option, not the default (why the default is centered).\nFIRE's own integrator is a separate setting.","title":"The call with every option written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#Why-the-dashpot-needs-a-choice","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"With a dashpot the acceleration depends on a velocity mathbf v^ast:\n\nmathbf a =\nfrac\nmathbf F(mathbf r) -\nunderbrace\ngammamathbf v^ast\n_textdashpot\nm\n\nThis page puts mathbf v^ast = mathbf v_n, the velocity at the start of the step, so nothing has to be solved or predicted.\nLagged velocity Verlet puts mathbf v^ast = mathbf v_n+12, and centered velocity Verlet puts mathbf v^ast = mathbf v_n+1 and solves for it.","title":"Why the dashpot needs a choice"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#Steps","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"There is no acceleration to hold over, because the force is computed from the stored state at the start of every step.\n\nCompute the acceleration at the current state, with the dashpot on the old velocity:\n\nmathbf a_n =\nfrac\nmathbf F(mathbf r_n) -\nunderbrace\ngammamathbf v_n\n_textold velocity\nm\n\nKick the velocity with the full step:\n\nmathbf v_n+1 =\nmathbf v_n +\nmathbf a_nDelta t\n\nDrift the positions with the new velocity:\n\nmathbf r_n+1 =\nmathbf r_n +\nunderbrace\nmathbf v_n+1\n_textnew velocity\nDelta t","title":"Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#Properties","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"Stable step: it is the same map as the lagged form for one damped oscillator, so it is stable up to\n\nomegaDelta t le\nunderbrace\n2\n_textundamped limit\nunderbrace\nleft(sqrt1+zeta^2 - zetaright)\n_textdamping shrinks it\n\nAccuracy: first order at every damping in the one-oscillator test, and about 2.5 to 10 times less accurate than lagged velocity Verlet at zeta = 005 and 001, with nearly the same error from zeta approx 03 up.\nCost: one force evaluation per step, with no half-step velocity to predict.\nRestart: the force is a function of the stored positions, velocities and contact history, so a restart stores only those.\n\nwarning: How semi-implicit Euler fails, and how SoftPack guards against it\nFailure mechanismsBlow-up above the stable step: the stable step shrinks as damping grows, to 027t_textcol for one contact at the default zeta = 098 and about 005t_textcol in the 3D packing worst case, and past it the run diverges.\nAccuracy: first order, so results change with Delta t more than for the centered form, and more than for lagged velocity Verlet at weak damping.\nExact restart: the restart test was run only for the lagged form, and the sliding flags may have to be stored for this integrator.How SoftPack mitigates thisThe run-time check compares Delta t with the stable step for this integrator, which includes the damping penalty, and warns when it is exceeded.\nWith near-critical contact damping the step fraction should stay at or below about 120.\nThe restart file records the integrator and refuses a restart with a different one.\nThe trade-offs: centered velocity Verlet is second order with no damping penalty at the cost of a solve every step, and lagged velocity Verlet is more accurate at weak damping at the cost of a half-step state in the restart file.","title":"Properties"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#Compared-with-the-other-two","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":" Centered (default) Lagged Semi-implicit Euler\nDashpot uses mathbf v_n+1 (implicit) mathbf v_n+12 mathbf v_n\nCost per step a linear solve one force evaluation one force evaluation\nStable step, one oscillator omegaDelta t le 2 omegaDelta t le 2left(sqrt1+zeta^2-zetaright) the same as lagged\nOrder, damped 2 1 1\nRestart stores mathbf r, mathbf v, contact history also mathbf a_n mathbf r, mathbf v, contact history","title":"Compared with the other two"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#Open-questions","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"Whether this option is worth keeping next to lagged velocity Verlet is open, because at moderate damping the one-oscillator test shows the same stable step and nearly the same error.\nWhether a restart is bitwise in frictional runs is not tested for this integrator.","title":"Open questions"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#Theory","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nSemi-implicit Euler for DampedDynamics: the same steps with every property.\nTime step with damping: the stable-step derivation.\nFIRE's integrator: the same scheme as FIRE's default.","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/#algorithm","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Algorithms and combinations; FIRE is the default algorithm; Any combination is allowed.\n\nMinimizer or dynamics; damping and drag live inside these, not in pack().\n\nDefault: FIRE()","title":"algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nFIRE() default\nDampedDynamics() ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/snapshots/#snapshots","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: pack(snapshots = …); Snapshots and movie.\n\nDefault: nothing","title":"snapshots"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/snapshots/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nSnapshots() \nnothing default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/drag/StokesDrag/#StokesDrag()","page":"StokesDrag()","text":"Up: drag\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: StokesDrag(velocity, spin, during); StokesDrag — always on.","title":"StokesDrag()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/drag/StokesDrag/#Options","page":"StokesDrag()","text":"velocity\nspin\nduring","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_count/#particles_count","page":"particles_count","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: 256 particles; Other particle counts, algorithm and friction.\n\nDefault: 256","title":"particles_count"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_count/#Choices","page":"particles_count","text":"Choice Default\n256 default\nany N ≥ 2 ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/custom_test/#Your-own-test-function","page":"Your own test function","text":"Up: convergence\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Any function.\n\nAny function my_test(state) that returns true when the packing is at rest at the current box. It receives a read-only RunState (fields) and is called at the end of every step. A true ends the current minimization, not the run, and the pressure check still follows.","title":"Your own test function"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/TwoContact/#TwoContact()","page":"TwoContact()","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: TwoContact(cone_test = …); TwoContact.","title":"TwoContact()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/TwoContact/#Options","page":"TwoContact()","text":"cone_test","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/Packing/#Packing","page":"Packing","text":"Up: pack()\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: The returned packing and the file; What a file holds; Run outcomes.\n\nWhat pack() returns: fields, converged, what to do with it.","title":"Packing"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/dimension/#dimension","page":"dimension","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Two dimensions; Three dimensions.\n\nDefault: 2","title":"dimension"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/dimension/#Choices","page":"dimension","text":"Choice Default\n2 default\n3 ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/#mass","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Mass.\n\nParticle masses.\n\nDefault: EqualMass()","title":"mass"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nEqualMass() default\nDensity() ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RelativeForce/#RelativeForce()","page":"RelativeForce()","text":"Up: convergence\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: RelativeForce(tolerance = …); Force and torque test (RelativeForce).\n\nThis is the default for convergence.","title":"RelativeForce()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RelativeForce/#Options","page":"RelativeForce()","text":"tolerance","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/CundallStrack/#CundallStrack()","page":"CundallStrack()","text":"Up: friction\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: CundallStrack(friction_coefficient = …); CundallStrack.","title":"CundallStrack()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/CundallStrack/#Options","page":"CundallStrack()","text":"friction_coefficient\nstiffness_ratio","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_packing/#restart_packing","page":"restart_packing","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: restart_packing = nothing; restart_packing = path; No restart by default (restart_packing).\n\nDefault: nothing","title":"restart_packing"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_packing/#Choices","page":"restart_packing","text":"Choice Default\nnothing default\npath to a file ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/Mindlin/#Mindlin()","page":"Mindlin()","text":"Up: friction\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Mindlin(friction_coefficient = …); Mindlin.","title":"Mindlin()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/Mindlin/#Options","page":"Mindlin()","text":"friction_coefficient\npoisson_ratio","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/compression/#compression","page":"compression","text":"Up: write_packing()\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: write_packing(compression = …); Compression (compression); gzip compression.","title":"compression"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/compression/#Choices","page":"compression","text":"Choice Default\nnothing default\n:gzip ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/IsotropicCompression/#IsotropicCompression()","page":"IsotropicCompression()","text":"Up: protocol\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Isotropic compression; pack(protocol = …); Compression of the default packing.\n\nShrink the box and every position by the same factor, minimize, repeat.\n\nThis is the default for protocol.","title":"IsotropicCompression()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/IsotropicCompression/#Options","page":"IsotropicCompression()","text":"strain_step","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/#contact","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: pack(contact = …); Hookean contacts; Hertzian contacts.\n\nContact law.\n\nDefault: Hookean()","title":"contact"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nHookean() default\nHertzian() ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/CollisionTime/#CollisionTime()","page":"CollisionTime()","text":"Up: timestep\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: timestep = CollisionTime(fraction = …); CollisionTime (default); Collision time of a Hookean contact.\n\nThis is the default for timestep.","title":"CollisionTime()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/CollisionTime/#Options","page":"CollisionTime()","text":"fraction","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/#target","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: pack(target = …); Target pressure.\n\nWhat to compress to.\n\nDefault: Pressure(1e-4)","title":"target"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nPressure() default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/Unresolved/#Unresolved()","page":"Unresolved()","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Unresolved(floor = …, cone_test = …); Unresolved.","title":"Unresolved()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/Unresolved/#Options","page":"Unresolved()","text":"floor\ncone_test","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/#boundary","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: pack(boundary = …); Periodic boundaries; Walls; Boundary, start and compression.\n\nPeriodic box or rigid walls; every side of the box scales by the same factor.\n\nDefault: Periodic()","title":"boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nPeriodic() default\nWalls() ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#Bidisperse()","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"Up: particles_size_distribution\n\nA mixture of two diameters: a fixed fraction of the particles are small, and the rest are larger by a fixed ratio. It is the default particles_size_distribution, and its small diameter is SoftPack's unit of length.","title":"Bidisperse()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#The-call-with-every-option-written-out","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"pack(                                           # run the packing\n    particles_size_distribution = Bidisperse(   # two-diameter mixture (default)\n        diameter_small = 1.0,                   # small diameter; sets the length unit (default)\n        diameter_ratio = 1.4,                   # large diameter divided by small diameter (default)\n        fraction_small = 0.5,                   # number fraction of small particles (default)\n    )                                           # end of Bidisperse call\n)                                               # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nparticles_size_distribution = Bidisperse(...) (default) — a mixture of two diameters in a fixed ratio, with N_s = fN particles small and f = fraction_small.\nThe two-size mixture prevents crystallization in 2D [OHern2003].\ndiameter_small = 1.0 (default) — the small diameter, which also sets the length unit.\n\nd_i in d_s rd_s qquad d_s = textttdiameter_small\n\n[OHern2003] \"The diameter of the smaller particle is denoted by σ\" … \"lengths are measured in units of σ.\"\n\ndiameter_ratio = 1.4 (default) — the ratio r = d_ld_s of the large to the small diameter.\n[OHern2003] 50-50 mixtures \"with a size ratio of 1.4 in order to prevent crystallization.\"\nfraction_small = 0.5 (default) — the number fraction of small particles.\n\nN_s = fN text small qquad N_l = (1-f)N text large qquad f = textttfraction_small\n\nThe polydispersity (standard deviation over mean) of the default is sqrtf(1-f)(r-1)(f+(1-f)r) = 16 = 0167 [OHern2003].","title":"The call with every option written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#Why-these-defaults","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"Why a mixture at all: equal disks crystallize in 2D, and a crystal is not the disordered packing jamming studies need.\nA second size frustrates the hexagonal order (SoftPack note).\ndiameter_small = 1.0 — the small diameter as the length unit.\n[OHern2003] \"The diameter of the smaller particle is denoted by σ.\" ... \"lengths are measured in units of σ\"\ndiameter_ratio = 1.4 and fraction_small = 0.5 — the [OHern2003] mixture.\n[OHern2003] \"In our two-dimensional (2D) simulations, we have used 50-50 mixtures of particles with a size ratio of 1.4 in order to prevent crystallization\".","title":"Why these defaults"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#Theory","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nBidisperse law\nBidisperse moments\nChanging the length unit\nBidisperse default\nWorked numbers","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#References","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#:lagged_velocity_verlet","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"Up: integrator\n\nVelocity Verlet with the dashpot evaluated on the half-step velocity mathbf v_n+12, which is how LAMMPS evaluates granular dashpots. It is explicit, with one force evaluation per step and no solve, but its stable step shrinks as damping grows and its restart has to store the forces and torques of the last evaluation.","title":":lagged_velocity_verlet"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#The-call-with-every-option-written-out","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"pack(                                           # run the packing\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(                 # damped dynamics instead of the default FIRE\n        integrator = :lagged_velocity_verlet,   # dashpot on the half-step velocity\n    )                                           # end of DampedDynamics call\n)                                               # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nalgorithm = DampedDynamics(...) — damped dynamics, Newton's equations with contact dashpots.\nintegrator = :lagged_velocity_verlet — the dashpot force uses mathbf v_n+12.\nThis is an option, not the default (why the default is centered).","title":"The call with every option written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#Why-the-dashpot-needs-a-choice","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"With a dashpot the new acceleration depends on a velocity mathbf v^ast:\n\nmathbf a_n+1 =\nfrac\nmathbf F(mathbf r_n+1) -\nunderbrace\ngammamathbf v^ast\n_textdashpot\nm\n\nVelocity Verlet computes mathbf v_n+1 from mathbf a_n+1, so mathbf v_n+1 does not exist yet when mathbf a_n+1 is needed.\nThis page puts mathbf v^ast = mathbf v_n+12, the velocity already known at that point.\nCentered velocity Verlet puts mathbf v^ast = mathbf v_n+1 and solves for it, and semi-implicit Euler puts mathbf v^ast = mathbf v_n.","title":"Why the dashpot needs a choice"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#Steps","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"Steps 0, 1 and 3 are the same as in undamped velocity Verlet.\n\nHold on to mathbf a_n from the previous step (same as undamped velocity Verlet).\nMove the positions (same as undamped velocity Verlet):\n\nmathbf r_n+1 =\nmathbf r_n +\nmathbf v_nDelta t +\ntfrac12mathbf a_nDelta t^2\n\nPredict the half-step velocity, then compute the new acceleration with it in the dashpot (differs from undamped velocity Verlet):\n\nmathbf v_n+12 =\nmathbf v_n + mathbf a_n\nunderbrace\nfracDelta t2\n_texthalf step\n\nmathbf a_n+1 =\nfrac\nmathbf F(mathbf r_n+1) -\nunderbrace\ngammamathbf v_n+12\n_texthalf-step velocity\nm\n\nFinish the velocity with both accelerations (same as undamped velocity Verlet):\n\nmathbf v_n+1 =\nmathbf v_n +\ntfrac12\nunderbrace\n(mathbf a_n + mathbf a_n+1)\n_textaverage acceleration\nDelta t","title":"Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#Properties","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"Stable step: one damped oscillator of angular frequency omega and damping ratio zeta is stable up to\n\nomegaDelta t le\nunderbrace\n2\n_textundamped limit\nunderbrace\nleft(sqrt1+zeta^2 - zetaright)\n_textdamping shrinks it\n\nThe same formula is the central-difference result with the damping term lagged [CodeAster].\nAccuracy: first order once damping is not weak, because the dashpot velocity is half a step old.\n[CodeAster] says the lagged approximation is valid for weak damping and loses second-order accuracy when damping is large.\n[Golshan2023] find velocity Verlet with a velocity-dependent force first order, and still more accurate than explicit Euler in practice.\nIn the one-oscillator test it was first order at zeta = 03 and 098, and 2.5 to 10 times more accurate than semi-implicit Euler at zeta = 005 and 001.\nCost: one force evaluation per step.\nRestart: mathbf a_n was built from mathbf v_n-12, so it cannot be rebuilt from (mathbf r_n mathbf v_n) and a restart has to store the forces and torques of the last evaluation.\n\nwarning: How the lagged velocity Verlet fails, and how SoftPack guards against it\nFailure mechanismsBlow-up above the stable step: the stable step shrinks as damping grows, to 027t_textcol for one contact at the default zeta = 098 and about 005t_textcol in the 3D packing worst case, and past it the run diverges.\nAccuracy: the half-step-old dashpot velocity makes the damped dynamics first order, so results change more with Delta t than for the centered form.\nHalf-step state: restarting from stored full-step velocities gives a different trajectory, as LAMMPS warns for granular styles, and even re-evaluating from stored half-step velocities was not bitwise in the frictional probe.\nStaggered variables: [Vyas2025] report unphysical contact behavior in the LAMMPS velocity Verlet at large size ratios, from mixing half-step and full-step variables, and whether this matters at the default diameter ratio 1.4 is untested.How SoftPack mitigates thisThe run-time check compares Delta t with the stable step for this integrator, which includes the damping penalty, and warns when it is exceeded.\nWith near-critical contact damping the step fraction should stay at or below about 120.\nThe restart file stores the forces and torques of the last evaluation, records the integrator and refuses a restart with a different one.\nThe trade-offs: centered velocity Verlet removes the damping penalty and is second order, at the cost of a solve every step, and semi-implicit Euler has the same stable step with no half-step state.","title":"Properties"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#Compared-with-the-other-two","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":" Centered (default) Lagged Semi-implicit Euler\nDashpot uses mathbf v_n+1 (implicit) mathbf v_n+12 mathbf v_n\nCost per step a linear solve one force evaluation one force evaluation\nStable step, one oscillator omegaDelta t le 2 omegaDelta t le 2left(sqrt1+zeta^2-zetaright) the same as lagged\nOrder, damped 2 1 1\nRestart stores mathbf r, mathbf v, contact history also mathbf a_n mathbf r, mathbf v, contact history","title":"Compared with the other two"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#Open-questions","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"Whether a restart from the stored forces and torques is bitwise for every damping setting is tested only on one frictional probe.\nWhether this integrator is worth keeping over semi-implicit Euler at moderate damping is open, because the one-oscillator test shows the same stable step and nearly the same error from zeta approx 03 up.","title":"Open questions"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#Theory","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nLagged velocity Verlet: the same steps with every property.\nTime step with damping: the stable-step derivation.\nThe half-step velocity: the LAMMPS restart caveat and the probe.","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#References","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"[CodeAster] CodeAster developers. *Algorithmes d'intégration directe de l'opérateur DYNAVIBRA / BASECALCUL='PHYS', manual R5.05.02*. <https://code-aster.org/doc/v16/manr/r5/r5.05.02.pdf>. Section 6.2, central differences. Accessed 2026-11-24.\n[Golshan2023] Shahab Golshan, Peter Münch, Rene Gassmöller, Martin Kronbichler, Bruno Blais (2023). Lethe-DEM: an open-source parallel discrete element solver with load balancing. Computational Particle Mechanics 10, 77–96. doi:10.1007/s40571-022-00478-6.\n[Vyas2025] Dhairya R. Vyas, Julio M. Ottino, Richard M. Lueptow, Paul B. Umbanhowar (2025). Improved velocity-Verlet algorithm for the discrete element method. Computer Physics Communications 310, 109524. doi:10.1016/j.cpc.2025.109524.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/drag/#drag","page":"Overview","text":"Up: DampedDynamics()\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: DampedDynamics(drag = …); No Stokes drag.\n\nStokes drag; FIRE() and DampedDynamics() both take this keyword.","title":"drag"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/drag/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nStokesDrag() \nnothing default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Monodisperse/#Monodisperse()","page":"Monodisperse()","text":"Up: particles_size_distribution\n\nEvery particle has the same diameter, and that diameter is the unit of length. It is not the default, because equal disks crystallize in 2D.","title":"Monodisperse()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Monodisperse/#The-call-with-every-option-written-out","page":"Monodisperse()","text":"pack(                                             # run the packing\n    particles_size_distribution = Monodisperse(   # every particle has the same diameter\n        diameter = 1.0,                           # the one diameter; sets the length unit (default)\n    )                                             # end of Monodisperse call\n)                                                 # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nparticles_size_distribution = Monodisperse(...) — every particle has the same diameter.\nEqual disks crystallize in 2D, which the bidisperse default avoids.\n[OHern2003] studied \"the same bidisperse mixture as well as monodisperse systems\" in 3D.\ndiameter = 1.0 (default) — the one diameter, which also sets the length unit.\nSoftPack choice: the one diameter is the length unit.","title":"The call with every option written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Monodisperse/#Theory","page":"Monodisperse()","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nMonodisperse law\nMonodisperse","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Monodisperse/#References","page":"Monodisperse()","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Periodic/#Periodic()","page":"Periodic()","text":"Up: boundary\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Periodic boundaries; Boundary, start and compression.\n\nPeriodic square (2D) or cube (3D) box.\n\nThis is the default for boundary.","title":"Periodic()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hookean/#Hookean()","page":"Hookean()","text":"Up: contact\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Hookean contacts; Contact law; Force and stiffness from the potential.\n\nThis is the default for contact.","title":"Hookean()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hookean/#Options","page":"Hookean()","text":"stiffness","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/#initial_state","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: pack(initial_state = …); Dilute start.\n\nWhere the compression starts.\n\nDefault: Dilute(packing_fraction = 0.25)","title":"initial_state"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nDilute() default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/movie/#movie","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: pack(movie = …); Snapshots and movie.\n\nDefault: nothing","title":"movie"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/movie/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nMovie() \nnothing default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/movie/Movie/#Movie()","page":"Movie()","text":"Up: movie\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: pack(movie = …); Snapshots and movie.","title":"Movie()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/movie/Movie/#Options","page":"Movie()","text":"path\nevery","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/RelativeVelocityDamping/#RelativeVelocityDamping()","page":"RelativeVelocityDamping()","text":"Up: damping\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: RelativeVelocityDamping(rate); RelativeVelocityDamping.","title":"RelativeVelocityDamping()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/RelativeVelocityDamping/#Options","page":"RelativeVelocityDamping()","text":"rate","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/pressure_tolerance/#pressure_tolerance","page":"pressure_tolerance","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: pressure_tolerance; Pressure test (pressure_tolerance).\n\nDefault: 1e-4\n\nThe pressure must be within this relative band of the target for every convergence choice, because the compression protocol checks it after every minimization.","title":"pressure_tolerance"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/pressure_tolerance/#Choices","page":"pressure_tolerance","text":"Choice Default\n1e-4 default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Creating-a-packing-with-pack()","page":"Overview","text":"Design decision source: design-references.md §0. Status: design stage (no engine code yet).","title":"Creating a packing with pack()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Summary","page":"Overview","text":"pack() with no arguments makes a 2D packing of 256 frictionless Hookean disks, a 50:50 bidisperse mixture with diameter ratio 1.4, compressed with FIRE to the pressure P = 10^-4, with rattlers removed. It starts from random, non-touching particles at packing fraction 0.25 in a periodic box and shrinks the box isotropically until the pressure reaches the target. Every setting below has a default and a reason. This page lists them all in one place: the default, what it means as an equation, the other choices, the main source, and the wiki section that explains it. Sources are cited by key and listed in full at the end; SoftPack choice marks a default with no literature source.\n\nUnits throughout: small diameter d = 1, contact stiffness k = 1, particle mass m = 1, so lengths are in d, forces in kd (Hookean), pressures in kd^2-D and times in tau = sqrtmk (Units and length unit). Symbols are defined in Notation.","title":"Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#The-default-packing-in-one-call","page":"Overview","text":"Every default written out:\n\npack(                                                                # run the packing with every default written out\n    dimension                   = 2,                                 # 2D packing of disks (use 3 for spheres)\n    particles_count             = 256,                               # number of particles\n    particles_size_distribution = Bidisperse(                        # two-diameter mixture\n        diameter_small = 1.0,                                        # small diameter; the length unit\n        diameter_ratio = 1.4,                                        # large diameter divided by small diameter\n        fraction_small = 0.5                                         # number fraction of small particles (50:50)\n    ),                                                               # end of Bidisperse call\n    particles_diameters         = nothing,                           # draw diameters from the distribution, not an explicit list\n    contact                     = Hookean(stiffness = 1.0),          # Hookean contact law, stiffness k = 1\n    friction                    = nothing,                           # frictionless contacts\n    mass                        = EqualMass(1.0),                    # every particle has mass 1\n    target                      = Pressure(1e-4),                    # compress until the contact pressure reaches 1e-4\n    boundary                    = Periodic(),                        # periodic square (2D) or cube (3D) box\n    initial_state               = Dilute(packing_fraction = 0.25),   # random start at packing fraction 0.25, no particles touching\n    protocol                    = IsotropicCompression(              # shrink the box in isotropic steps\n        strain_step = 1e-2                                           # the box side shrinks by 1% in the first step\n    ),                                                               # end of IsotropicCompression call\n    convergence                 = RelativeForce(tolerance = 1e-8),   # stop when net forces are below 1e-8 of the mean contact force\n    pressure_tolerance          = 1e-4,                              # relative pressure tolerance\n    steps_max                   = 10^7,                              # maximum number of steps per call\n    failure_action              = :warn,                             # warn and return the packing if not converged\n    restart_packing             = nothing,                           # start a fresh run, no restart\n    restart_file                = nothing,                           # no periodic saves during the run\n    algorithm                   = FIRE(),                            # FIRE with LAMMPS FIRE 2.0 defaults, no damping, no drag\n    timestep                    = CollisionTime(fraction = 1/50),    # time step is 1/50 of the collision time\n    rattlers                    = ContactCount(cone_test = true),    # remove particles with too few contacts, cone test on\n    seed                        = 1,                                 # random seed\n    snapshots                   = nothing,                           # no snapshots recorded\n    movie                       = nothing,                           # no movie rendered\n)                                                                    # end of pack call\n\nand, for files, write_packing(path, packing; rattlers_file = false, compression = nothing), which writes HDF5 (file format).\n\npack(...) — runs the packing with every default written out.\nEach keyword is explained in the tables below: its default, meaning, other choices, main source and a link to the section with the reasons.\nParticles, contacts, mass and target: Particles and contacts.\nBox, start and compression: Box, start and compression.\nConvergence, failure and restarts: Stopping, failure and restarts.\nAlgorithm and time step: Algorithm and time step.\nDamped dynamics: Damping (damped dynamics).\nRattlers: Rattlers.\nSeed and outputs: Reproducibility and outputs.\nwrite_packing(path, packing; rattlers_file = false, compression = nothing) — the file call, written with its defaults (file format).\nrattlers_file = false — write only the remaining particles (rattlers_file).\ncompression = nothing — leave the datasets uncompressed (compression).","title":"The default packing in one call"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Three-ways-to-call-pack()","page":"Overview","text":"pack()                                                  # every default\npack(particles_count = 2000, target = Pressure(1e-4))   # keywords\npack(params)                                            # a reusable PackParams value\n\npack() — runs with every default.\npack(particles_count = 2000, target = Pressure(1e-4)) — runs with the keywords you pass, and every keyword you leave out keeps its default.\npack(params) — runs with a reusable settings value, described on the PackParams page.\npack() returns a Packing, described on the Packing page.","title":"Three ways to call pack()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Where-next","page":"Overview","text":"Available options: every keyword of pack() in one place, one link each, with the choices of each keyword.\nwrite_packing(): saving the packing that pack() returns.\nTheory pages: the models, derivations and worked numbers behind the defaults.","title":"Where next"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Particles-and-contacts","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\ndimension 2 disks, D = 2 3 (spheres) SoftPack choice dimension\nparticles_count 256 N = 256 any N ge 2 SoftPack choice particles_count\nparticles_size_distribution Bidisperse(diameter_small = 1.0, diameter_ratio = 1.4, fraction_small = 0.5) half the particles have d = 1, half 14d UniformPolydisperse(diameter_mean = 1.0, spread = 0.2): d_i uniform on 08 12; Monodisperse(diameter = 1.0) [OHern2003]; [Somfai2005] for the uniform law particles_size_distribution\nparticles_diameters nothing diameters drawn from the distribution a vector, one diameter per particle SoftPack choice particles_diameters\ncontact Hookean(stiffness = 1.0) U(delta) = tfrac12 kdelta^2, f = kdelta while the overlap delta  0 Hertzian(stiffness = 1.0): U = tfrac25 kdelta^52, f = kdelta^32 [OHern2003] contact\nfriction nothing frictionless: normal forces only CundallStrack, Mindlin (next table) SoftPack choice friction\nmass EqualMass(1.0) m_i = 1 for every particle Density(1.0; shape = :disc): m_i = rhopi d_i^24 (:sphere in 3D: rhopi d_i^36) SoftPack choice; [LAMMPSset] for density masses mass\ntarget Pressure(1e-4) P = dfrac1DOmegasum_c mathbf f^ccdotboldsymbolell^c = 10^-4 (boldsymbolell^c the branch vector, Omega the box area or volume), elastic forces only any pressure [Agnolin2007] (stress formula) target\n\nWith friction on, the friction models' own defaults:\n\nSetting Default Meaning Main source Details\nCundallStrack(friction_coefficient = 0.5, stiffness_ratio = :matched) mu = 05, k_tk_n = 1(1 + mR^2I) linear tangential spring capped at lvert F_t^textelrvert le mu F_n^textel; k_tk_n = 13 for disks, 27 for spheres [Somfai2005], [Silbert2001]; [Santos2024] for the model CundallStrack()\nMindlin(friction_coefficient = 0.5, poisson_ratio = 0.0) mu = 05, nu = 0 Hertz–Mindlin tangential force (needs Hertzian); nu = 0 gives k_t = k_n [Somfai2005], [Shundyak2007] Mindlin()","title":"Particles and contacts"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Box,-start-and-compression","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\nboundary Periodic() periodic square (2D) or cube (3D) box of side L, every side scaled by the same factor Walls(friction = true): rigid flat walls, with the contact law and, when friction is set, its friction model; Walls(friction = false) for normal forces only [Agnolin2007] (periodic cell); SoftPack choice (square or cube, walls) boundary\ninitial_state Dilute(packing_fraction = 0.25) random particles at varphi_0 = 025, at rest, placed by random sequential addition so that none touches any varphi_0 at which the particles fit [Agnolin2007] (loose start); SoftPack choice (value) initial_state\nprotocol IsotropicCompression(strain_step = 1e-2) shrink the box by 1% per step and minimize; accept a step below the pressure band, reject and halve the step above it any strain_step SoftPack choice protocol","title":"Box, start and compression"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Stopping,-failure-and-restarts","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\nconvergence RelativeForce(tolerance = 1e-8) max_i lvertmathbf F_irvert  epsilon_Flangle f_nrangle and, with friction, max_i lvertmathbf T_irvert  epsilon_Flangle f_nranglelangle drangle, with epsilon_F = 10^-8 (the rest test only; the pressure row below applies to every option) LeidenAcceleration(tolerance = 1e-10), KineticEnergy(tolerance = 1e-8), RestartTest(), any function [Agnolin2007], [Gilabert2007] convergence\npressure_tolerance 1e-4 lvert P - P_texttargetrvert le epsilon_P P_texttarget, epsilon_P = 10^-4, checked after any convergence option any value [Agnolin2007] pressure_tolerance\nsteps_max 10^7 at most 10^7 steps per call any value SoftPack choice steps_max\nfailure_action :warn warn and return the packing with converged = false :error SoftPack choice failure_action\nrestart_packing nothing start from scratch path to a file written by write_packing [LAMMPSreadrestart] (precedent) restart_packing\nrestart_file nothing no state saved during the run RestartFile(path; every = interval), every required [LAMMPSrestart] (precedent) restart_file\n\nThe options' own defaults: LeidenAcceleration from [Somfai2005], KineticEnergy (mean kinetic energy per particle below epsilon_K P d^D) from [Agnolin2007] and [Xiong2019], RestartTest (zero the velocities, check the forces do not grow back) from [Agnolin2007] (§5).","title":"Stopping, failure and restarts"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Algorithm-and-time-step","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\nalgorithm FIRE() FIRE 2.0 minimization with the LAMMPS defaults (next table) DampedDynamics(): mathbf Mddotmathbf r = mathbf F(mathbf r) - boldsymbolGammadotmathbf r [Bitzek2006], [Guenole2020] algorithm\ntimestep CollisionTime(fraction = 1/50) Delta t = t_textcol50, with t_textcol = pisqrtM_nk_n for the lightest pair and stiffest contact (undamped Hooke) any fraction, or a fixed number [Silbert2001] timestep\n\nFIRE's parameters (§14), all from the LAMMPS FIRE 2.0 defaults in [Guenole2020] (Table 1), with Delta t_textstart the step from timestep:\n\nFIRE setting Default Meaning Details\ntimestep_max 10 Delta t_textmax = 10Delta t_textstart FIRE()\ntimestep_min 0.02 Delta t_textmin = 002Delta t_textstart FIRE()\ndelay 20 N_textdelay = 20 downhill steps before the step may grow FIRE()\ntimestep_grow 1.1 Delta t leftarrow min(11Delta t Delta t_textmax) FIRE()\ntimestep_shrink 0.5 Delta t leftarrow 05Delta t after an uphill move, if that stays ge Delta t_textmin FIRE()\nmixing_coefficient 0.25 alpha_textstart = 025 in mathbf v leftarrow (1-alpha)mathbf v + alphalvertmathbf vrverthatmathbf F FIRE()\nmixing_shrink 0.99 alpha leftarrow 099alpha on each growth step FIRE()\nuphill_max 2000 stop, unconverged, after 2000 consecutive uphill moves FIRE()\ninertia_correction true move back half a step after an uphill move (LAMMPS halfstepback) FIRE()\ndelay_initial true apply the delay at the start of the run too FIRE()\nintegrator :semi_implicit_euler semi-implicit (symplectic) Euler; :velocity_verlet is the option FIRE()\ndamping, drag nothing, nothing FIRE removes energy by its own velocity rules FIRE()","title":"Algorithm and time step"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Damping-(damped-dynamics)","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Main source Details\ndamping ContactDamping(normal = 0.98, tangential = 0.98) gamma_n = 098 cdot 2sqrtk_n M_n, gamma_t = 098 cdot 2sqrtk_t M_t [Agnolin2007] (0.98), [Gilabert2007] (ratio form), [Somfai2005] (both directions) ContactDamping()\ntangential_mass :with_rotation 1M_t = 1m_i + 1m_j + R_i^2I_i + R_j^2I_j (M_t = M_n3 for equal disks) SoftPack choice ContactDamping()\nattractive false F_n = max(0 k_ndelta - gamma_n v_n) [Somfai2005], [LAMMPSgran] ContactDamping()\ncoulomb_limit :elastic lvert F_t^textelrvert le mu F_n^textel; tangential dashpot off while sliding [Agnolin2007] ContactDamping()\ndrag nothing no Stokes drag (a = 0 in boldsymbolGamma = amathbf M + bmathbf K_0) SoftPack choice drag\nintegrator :centered_velocity_verlet the dashpot uses the end-of-step velocity mathbf v_n+1 (implicit); the options are :lagged_velocity_verlet (mathbf v_n+12) and :semi_implicit_euler (mathbf v_n) SoftPack choice integrator\n\nWith these defaults and Hookean contacts the damping is exactly stiffness-proportional, boldsymbolGamma = bmathbf K_0 with b = 2zetaomega_c, so every underdamped mode decays at beta_ell = bomega_ell^22 (§8).\n\nThe options' own defaults: ViscousRate(normal = 0.5, tangential = 0.5), rates r = 05tau with gamma = m_texteffr [Santos2024]; RelativeVelocityDamping(rate = 0.5), mathbf F_ij = -gamma(mathbf v_i - mathbf v_j) (the CD model of [Vagberg2014]); StokesDrag(velocity = rate), mathbf F = -a_v mmathbf v, with no default rate (§8).","title":"Damping (damped dynamics)"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Rattlers","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\nrattlers ContactCount(cone_test = true) repeatedly remove particles with fewer than D + 1 contacts (frictionless) or fewer than 2 (frictional); with the cone test, also remove two-contact particles that cannot be in balance TwoContact(), ForceFloor(floor = 1e-3), Unresolved(floor = :tolerance), nothing, any function [Xiong2019], [Gilabert2007] rattlers\n\nThe options' sources: TwoContact [Ishima2023], ForceFloor [Shundyak2007], Unresolved [Agnolin2007] (§9).","title":"Rattlers"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Reproducibility-and-outputs","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\nseed 1 random numbers from StableRNG(1), the same stream on every machine and Julia version any integer (StableRNG accepts 0 le textseed le 2^64 - 1) SoftPack choice (value); [StableRNGs], [JuliaRandom] (generator) seed\nfile format (write_packing) HDF5 one binary file, read natively by Julia, Python and MATLAB CSV [HDF5jl], [h5py], [MATLABh5read] path\nwrite_packing rattlers_file false the file holds the packing without rattlers only true: a second file with every particle SoftPack choice rattlers_file\nwrite_packing compression nothing uncompressed, so datasets can be memory-mapped :gzip (level 4) [HDF5jl], [h5py] compression\nsnapshots, movie nothing, nothing nothing recorded during the run Snapshots(every = interval), Movie(path; every = interval) SoftPack choice snapshots, movie","title":"Reproducibility and outputs"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.\n[Bitzek2006] Erik Bitzek, Pekka Koskinen, Franz Gähler, Michael Moseler, Peter Gumbsch (2006). Structural Relaxation Made Simple. Physical Review Letters 97, 170201. doi:10.1103/physrevlett.97.170201.\n[Gilabert2007] F. A. Gilabert, J.-N. Roux, A. Castellanos (2007). Computer simulation of model cohesive powders: Influence of assembling procedure and contact laws on low consolidation states. Physical Review E 75, 011303. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.011303.\n[Guenole2020] Julien Guénolé, Wolfram G. Nöhring, Aviral Vaid, Frédéric Houllé, Zhuocheng Xie, Aruna Prakash, Erik Bitzek (2020). Assessment and optimization of the fast inertial relaxation engine (fire) for energy minimization in atomistic simulations and its implementation in lammps. Computational Materials Science 175, 109584. doi:10.1016/j.commatsci.2020.109584.\n[h5py] The h5py developers. Datasets, h5py documentation. https://docs.h5py.org/en/stable/high/dataset.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[HDF5jl] JuliaIO. HDF5.jl documentation. https://juliaio.github.io/HDF5.jl/stable/. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[Ishima2023] Daisuke Ishima, Kuniyasu Saitoh, Michio Otsuki, Hisao Hayakawa (2023). Theory of rigidity and numerical analysis of density of states of two-dimensional amorphous solids with dispersed frictional grains in the linear response regime. Physical Review E 107, 054902. doi:10.1103/physreve.107.054902.\n[JuliaRandom] The Julia Project. Random Numbers, The Julia Language manual (Reproducibility section). https://docs.julialang.org/en/v1/stdlib/Random/. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[LAMMPSgran] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: pair_style gran/hooke, gran/hooke/history, gran/hertz/history. https://docs.lammps.org/pair_gran.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[LAMMPSreadrestart] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: read_restart command. https://docs.lammps.org/read_restart.html. Accessed 2026-11-19.\n[LAMMPSrestart] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: restart command. https://docs.lammps.org/restart.html. Accessed 2026-11-19.\n[LAMMPSset] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: set command. https://docs.lammps.org/set.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[MATLABh5read] The MathWorks. h5read: Read data from HDF5 dataset, MATLAB documentation. https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/h5read.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.\n[Santos2024] A. P. Santos, Ishan Srivastava, Leonardo E. Silbert, Jeremy B. Lechman, Gary S. Grest (2024). Protocol-dependent frictional granular jamming simulations: cyclical, compression, and expansion. Frontiers in Soft Matter 3, 1326756. doi:10.3389/frsfm.2023.1326756.\n[Shundyak2007] Kostya Shundyak, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2007). Force mobilization and generalized isostaticity in jammed packings of frictional grains. Physical Review E 75, 010301. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.010301.\n[Silbert2001] Leonardo E. Silbert, Deniz Ertaş, Gary S. Grest, Thomas C. Halsey, Dov Levine, Steven J. Plimpton (2001). Granular flow down an inclined plane: Bagnold scaling and rheology. Physical Review E 64, 051302. doi:10.1103/physreve.64.051302.\n[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301.\n[StableRNGs] JuliaRandom. StableRNGs.jl. https://github.com/JuliaRandom/StableRNGs.jl. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[Vagberg2014] Daniel Vågberg, Peter Olsson, S. Teitel (2014). Dissipation and Rheology of Sheared Soft-Core Frictionless Disks Below Jamming. Physical Review Letters 112, 208303. doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.112.208303. arXiv:1311.4902.\n[Xiong2019] Fansheng Xiong, Philip Wang, Abram H. Clark, Thibault Bertrand, Nicholas T. Ouellette, Mark D. Shattuck, Corey S. O’Hern (2019). Comparison of shear and compression jammed packings of frictional disks. Granular Matter 21, 109. doi:10.1007/s10035-019-0964-9.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/LeidenAcceleration/#LeidenAcceleration()","page":"LeidenAcceleration()","text":"Up: convergence\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: LeidenAcceleration(tolerance = …); LeidenAcceleration.","title":"LeidenAcceleration()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/LeidenAcceleration/#Options","page":"LeidenAcceleration()","text":"tolerance","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Walls/#Walls()","page":"Walls()","text":"Up: boundary\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Walls; Pressure with walls.\n\nRigid flat walls; wall contacts use the contact law and, with friction set, its friction model.","title":"Walls()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Walls/#Options","page":"Walls()","text":"friction","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/snapshots/Snapshots/#Snapshots()","page":"Snapshots()","text":"Up: snapshots\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: pack(snapshots = …); Snapshots and movie.","title":"Snapshots()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/snapshots/Snapshots/#Options","page":"Snapshots()","text":"every","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#particles_size_distribution","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nparticles_size_distribution chooses the law that the particle diameters are drawn from. The default is a 50:50 mixture of two diameters in the ratio 1.4, the mixture that prevents crystallization in 2D jamming studies. Each choice fixes the length unit through its own defaults: Bidisperse() makes the small diameter 1, UniformPolydisperse() makes the mean diameter 1, and Monodisperse() makes its one diameter 1. Every output stores each particle's diameter.","title":"particles_size_distribution"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#The-call-with-the-default-written-out","page":"Overview","text":"pack(                                           # run the packing\n    particles_size_distribution = Bidisperse()  # two-diameter mixture (default)\n)                                               # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nparticles_size_distribution = Bidisperse() (default) — a 50:50 mixture of two diameters in the ratio 1.4.\nFor details, see Bidisperse().","title":"The call with the default written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Draws diameters from Length unit \nBidisperse() two diameters, large over small 1.4, half of each by number the small diameter is 1 default\nUniformPolydisperse() a uniform law between 0.8 and 1.2 times the mean the mean diameter is 1 \nMonodisperse() one diameter for every particle that diameter is 1 ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#Related-keywords","page":"Overview","text":"particles_diameters: give the diameters themselves instead of drawing them from a distribution.\nparticles_count: the number of particles N.\nUnits and length unit: how the length unit sets every other unit.","title":"Related keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#Theory","page":"Overview","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nParticle size distribution and length unit: the whole wiki page for this keyword.\nModel: the diameters, each law and the packing fraction of a mixture.\nDerivations: the moments of each law and how a change of length unit changes the numbers.","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/#restart_file","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: restart_file = nothing; restart_file = RestartFile(...); No saves by default (restart_file).\n\nPeriodic saves.\n\nDefault: nothing","title":"restart_file"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nRestartFile() \nnothing default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/#friction","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Friction models; Frictionless contacts.\n\nTangential forces.\n\nDefault: nothing","title":"friction"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nCundallStrack() \nMindlin() \nnothing default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/path/#path","page":"path","text":"Up: write_packing()\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: write_packing file format; File format: HDF5; CSV.\n\nFile format follows the extension.","title":"path"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/path/#Choices","page":"path","text":"Choice Default\nHDF5 .h5 default\nCSV .csv \nlater: XYZ, LAMMPS data ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/RestartFile/#RestartFile()","page":"RestartFile()","text":"Up: restart_file\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: restart_file = RestartFile(...); Save during the run (restart_file = RestartFile(...)).","title":"RestartFile()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/RestartFile/#Options","page":"RestartFile()","text":"path\nevery","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/#damping","page":"Overview","text":"Up: DampedDynamics()\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: DampedDynamics(damping = …); Normal and Tangential Contact Damping; FIRE(damping = …, drag = …).\n\nWhich dashpot model the algorithm uses; FIRE() and DampedDynamics() both take this keyword.","title":"damping"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nContactDamping() default\nViscousRate() \nRelativeVelocityDamping() \nnothing (FIRE default) ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/#timestep","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Time step; timestep = CollisionTime(fraction = …); timestep = fixed_value.\n\nDefault: CollisionTime()","title":"timestep"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nCollisionTime() default\nfixed number ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/Pressure/#Pressure()","page":"Pressure()","text":"Up: target\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Target pressure; Stress and pressure.\n\nThis is the default for target.","title":"Pressure()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/Pressure/#Options","page":"Pressure()","text":"pressure","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/custom_rule/#Your-own-rule-function","page":"Your own rule function","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: No removal and custom rules.\n\nAny function of the contact network, one true/false per particle.","title":"Your own rule function"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/#rattlers","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Rattler rules.\n\nWhich particles to remove.\n\nDefault: ContactCount()","title":"rattlers"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nContactCount() default\nTwoContact() \nForceFloor() \nUnresolved() \nYour own rule function \nfind_rattlers() \nnothing ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#particles_diameters","page":"particles_diameters","text":"Up: Available options\n\nGive the diameters themselves instead of drawing them from particles_size_distribution. By default no list is given and the diameters are drawn from the distribution.","title":"particles_diameters"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#The-call-with-the-default-written-out","page":"particles_diameters","text":"pack(                                # run the packing\n    particles_diameters = nothing,   # draw diameters from the size distribution (default)\n)                                    # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nparticles_diameters = nothing (default) — the diameters are drawn from particles_size_distribution.","title":"The call with the default written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#Giving-the-diameters","page":"particles_diameters","text":"pack(                                            # run the packing with given diameters\n    particles_diameters = [1.0, 1.4, 1.0, 1.4]   # exactly these diameters, one per particle; particles_count is then 4\n)                                                # end of pack call\n\nparticles_diameters = [1.0, 1.4, 1.0, 1.4] — exactly these diameters, one per particle.\nparticles_count is then the number of diameters, here 4.\nGive a distribution or the diameters, not both.\nThe length unit is then whatever unit the given diameters are in.\nWith k = 1 the pressure unit follows from it.","title":"Giving the diameters"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#Why-this-default","page":"particles_diameters","text":"SoftPack choice: an exact list is how a packing is matched to an experiment or to another code's particles.","title":"Why this default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#Theory","page":"particles_diameters","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nGiven diameter list\nGiven diameters","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/seed/#seed","page":"seed","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: seed; Seed (seed).\n\nDefault: 1","title":"seed"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/seed/#Choices","page":"seed","text":"Choice Default\n1 default\nany integer ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ForceFloor/#ForceFloor()","page":"ForceFloor()","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: ForceFloor(floor = …, cone_test = …); ForceFloor.","title":"ForceFloor()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ForceFloor/#Options","page":"ForceFloor()","text":"floor\ncone_test","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/Dilute/#Dilute()","page":"Dilute()","text":"Up: initial_state\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Dilute start; pack(initial_state = …).\n\nRandom particles at rest, none touching.\n\nThis is the default for initial_state.","title":"Dilute()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/Dilute/#Options","page":"Dilute()","text":"packing_fraction","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/#Saving-a-packing-with-write_packing()","page":"Overview","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\npack() returns a Packing in memory and writes no file. write_packing() is the separate call that writes one Packing to disk. Every option has a default, so the shortest call is the path and the packing.","title":"Saving a packing with write_packing()"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/#The-call-with-every-default-written-out","page":"Overview","text":"write_packing(                  # write one Packing to disk\n    \"my_packing.h5\",            # output path; the extension picks the format, .h5 is HDF5\n    my_packing;                 # the Packing returned by pack(); the ; starts the keyword arguments\n    rattlers_file = false,      # write only the remaining particles (default)\n    compression   = nothing     # leave the datasets uncompressed (default)\n)                               # end of write_packing call\n\nwrite_packing(...) — writes one Packing to disk, as a call separate from pack().\nFor details, see the Packing page.\n\"my_packing.h5\" — the output path, whose extension picks the format.\nHDF5 is the default format, read natively by Julia, Python and MATLAB.\nFor details, see path and file format.\nmy_packing — the Packing to write.\nThe ; ends the positional arguments and starts the keyword arguments (Julia syntax).\nrattlers_file = false (default) — write only the particles that remain after the rattler rule has run.\nWith true, a second file also holds every particle with a rattler flag.\nFor details, see rattlers_file.\ncompression = nothing (default) — leave the datasets uncompressed, so they can be memory-mapped.\nWith :gzip, the datasets are compressed with lossless gzip at level 4.\nFor details, see compression.","title":"The call with every default written out"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/#Where-next","page":"Overview","text":"path and file format: HDF5 and CSV.\nrattlers_file: the second file with every particle.\ncompression: gzip.\npack(): creating the packing to write.","title":"Where next"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/failure_action/#failure_action","page":"failure_action","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: failure_action = :warn; failure_action = :error; Warn and return (failure_action).\n\nDefault: :warn","title":"failure_action"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/failure_action/#Choices","page":"failure_action","text":"Choice Default\n:warn default\n:error ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#UniformPolydisperse()","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"Up: particles_size_distribution\n\nDiameters drawn uniformly around a mean, with the mean diameter as the unit of length. It is not the default: use it to match the Leiden frictional packings [Somfai2005], [Shundyak2007].","title":"UniformPolydisperse()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#The-call-with-every-option-written-out","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"pack(                                                    # run the packing\n    particles_size_distribution = UniformPolydisperse(   # diameters uniform around a mean\n        diameter_mean = 1.0,                             # mean diameter; sets the length unit (default)\n        spread        = 0.2,                             # half-width over the mean (default)\n    )                                                    # end of UniformPolydisperse call\n)                                                        # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nparticles_size_distribution = UniformPolydisperse(...) — diameters drawn uniformly between d_textmean(1-s) and d_textmean(1+s).\n\np(d) = frac12sd_textmean quad textfor  d_textmean(1-s) le d le d_textmean(1+s)\n\ndiameter_mean = 1.0 (default) — the mean diameter, which also sets the length unit: langle drangle = d_textmean.\n[Somfai2005] \"Our unit of length is the average grain diameter.\"\n[Shundyak2007] \"our units are such that the mass density, the average particle diameter, and the Young's modulus of the grains are 1.\"\nspread = 0.2 (default) — the half-width over the mean, s = textttspread.\nThe polydispersity (standard deviation over mean) is ssqrt3.\nThe diameters stay positive for s  1 (SoftPack note).\n[Somfai2005] \"For the 2D simulations the spheres have polydispersity to avoid crystallization: the diameters are uniformly distributed between 0.8 and 1.2 times their average.\"","title":"The call with every option written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#Why-this-option","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"It matches the Leiden frictional packings ([Somfai2005], [Shundyak2007]).\nTheir 2D particles are spheres for the mass (see mass).","title":"Why this option"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#Theory","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nUniform polydisperse law\nUniform moments\nChanging the length unit","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#References","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"[Shundyak2007] Kostya Shundyak, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2007). Force mobilization and generalized isostaticity in jammed packings of frictional grains. Physical Review E 75, 010301. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.010301.\n[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#:centered_velocity_verlet","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"Up: integrator\n\nVelocity Verlet with the dashpot evaluated on the end-of-step velocity mathbf v_n+1, so the velocity update is implicit. It is the default integrator. It costs a linear solve over the contacts every step, and in return its stable step does not shrink as damping grows.","title":":centered_velocity_verlet"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#The-call-with-every-option-written-out","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"pack(                                             # run the packing\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(                   # damped dynamics instead of the default FIRE\n        integrator = :centered_velocity_verlet,   # dashpot on the end-of-step velocity (default)\n    )                                             # end of DampedDynamics call\n)                                                 # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nalgorithm = DampedDynamics(...) — damped dynamics, Newton's equations with contact dashpots.\nintegrator = :centered_velocity_verlet (default) — the dashpot force uses mathbf v_n+1.\nSoftPack choice: the most accurate of the three, with a stable step that does not shrink with damping (why).","title":"The call with every option written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#Why-the-dashpot-needs-a-choice","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"With a dashpot the new acceleration depends on a velocity mathbf v^ast:\n\nmathbf a_n+1 =\nfrac\nmathbf F(mathbf r_n+1) -\nunderbrace\ngammamathbf v^ast\n_textdashpot\nm\n\nVelocity Verlet computes mathbf v_n+1 from mathbf a_n+1, so mathbf v_n+1 does not exist yet when mathbf a_n+1 is needed.\nLagged velocity Verlet puts mathbf v^ast = mathbf v_n+12, and semi-implicit Euler puts mathbf v^ast = mathbf v_n.\nThis page puts mathbf v^ast = mathbf v_n+1 and solves for it.","title":"Why the dashpot needs a choice"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#Steps","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"Hold on to mathbf a_n from the previous step (same as lagged velocity Verlet).\nMove the positions (same as lagged velocity Verlet):\n\nmathbf r_n+1 =\nmathbf r_n +\nmathbf v_nDelta t +\ntfrac12mathbf a_nDelta t^2\n\nPredict the half-step velocity (same as lagged velocity Verlet; the undamped scheme has no half-step velocity):\n\nmathbf v_n+12 =\nmathbf v_n + mathbf a_n\nunderbrace\nfracDelta t2\n_texthalf step\n\nFinish the velocity with the second half kick. mathbf v_n+1 is the unknown here, and it appears on both sides through the dashpot (differs from the other two):\n\nmathbf v_n+1 =\nmathbf v_n+12 +\nunderbrace\nfracDelta t2\n_texthalf step\n\nunderbrace\nmathbf a_n+1\n_textnew acceleration\n\nThe kick is the same as the last step of the undamped scheme, because mathbf v_n+12 = mathbf v_n + tfrac12mathbf a_nDelta t.\nPut mathbf a_n+1 in with the dashpot on the end velocity, so the unknown shows up on the right:\n\nmathbf v_n+1 =\nmathbf v_n+12 +\nunderbrace\nfracDelta t2\n_texthalf step\n\nfrac\nmathbf F(mathbf r_n+1) -\nunderbrace\ngammamathbf v_n+1\n_textdashpot on end velocity\nm\n\nMove the dashpot term to the left and factor out mathbf v_n+1:\n\nunderbrace\nleft(1 + fracgammaDelta t2mright)\n_textdamping factor\nmathbf v_n+1 =\nmathbf v_n+12 +\nunderbrace\nfracDelta t2mmathbf F(mathbf r_n+1)\n_textforce kick\n\nOne particle: divide by the damping factor, which is larger than 1, so damping shrinks the new velocity.\n\nmathbf v_n+1 =\nfrac\nmathbf v_n+12 + textforce kick\n\ntextdamping factor\n\n\nMany particles: multiply the factored equation by m first, so the mass is explicit.\n\nunderbrace\nleft(m + tfracDelta t2gammaright)\n_textone number\nmathbf v_n+1 =\nmmathbf v_n+12 +\ntfracDelta t2mathbf F(mathbf r_n+1)\n\nThen swap each symbol for its many-particle version.\nm to mathbf M\n, the masses on the diagonal.\ngamma to boldsymbolGamma\n, the damping matrix, which has entries linking particles in contact because a contact's dashpot depends on both particles' velocities.\nmathbf v\nand mathbf F now hold every particle's values in one list.\n\nunderbrace\nleft(mathbf M + tfracDelta t2boldsymbolGammaright)\n_textmatrix\nmathbf v_n+1 =\nmathbf Mmathbf v_n+12 +\ntfracDelta t2mathbf F(mathbf r_n+1)\n\nThe damping factor became a matrix, and a matrix cannot be divided into both sides, so mathbf v_n+1 comes from a linear solve every step.\nThe matrix is sparse, with entries only for particles that touch.\n\nNew acceleration, held for step 0 of the next step (new step; the other two integrators get mathbf a_n+1 in step 2):\n\nmathbf a_n+1 =\nfrac\nmathbf F(mathbf r_n+1) -\nunderbrace\ngammamathbf v_n+1\n_textend velocity\nm","title":"Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#Properties","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"Stable step: one damped oscillator of angular frequency omega is stable for every damping ratio zeta up to\n\nomegaDelta t le\nunderbrace\n2\n_textno damping penalty\n\nA packing is stable up to Delta t le 2omega_textmax, with omega_textmax its highest mode frequency.\nFor ContactDamping() with its defaults the damping is stiffness-proportional, so the modes decouple and each obeys the one-oscillator bound (SoftPack note).\nIn a test on 24-degree-of-freedom networks the limit stayed at 2omega_textmax for four random non-proportional damping matrices too, which is a test and not a proof.\nAccuracy: second order in the one-oscillator test at zeta = 001, 005, 03 and 098.\nCost: one linear solve over the contacts per step, plus one force evaluation.\nRestart: mathbf a_n is a function of (mathbf r_n mathbf v_n) and the contact history, so a restart stores only those.\n\nwarning: How the centered velocity Verlet fails, and how SoftPack guards against it\nFailure mechanismsBlow-up above the stable step: when omega_textmaxDelta t exceeds 2 the highest mode flips sign and grows every step, and the run diverges.\nCost: the solve every step can make a run slower than an explicit integrator even though the step may be longer, as [Samiei2013] found for implicit integration in DEM.\nNonlinear contacts: the bound is for linear damping, and Hertzian and Mindlin contacts, clipped dashpots and the Coulomb limit change the matrices from step to step, so for them it is an estimate.\nExact restart: the restart test passed only for the lagged form so far, and sliding flags may have to be stored for this integrator too.How SoftPack mitigates thisThe run-time check compares Delta t with the stable step of the chosen integrator and warns when it is exceeded; it does not change the step.\nThe default step keeps a margin of about 13 or more over the stable step in the 3D worst case.\nThe restart file records the integrator and refuses a restart with a different one.\nThe trade-offs: lagged velocity Verlet is explicit and needs no solve but its stable step shrinks as damping grows, and semi-implicit Euler is the cheapest but first order.","title":"Properties"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#Compared-with-the-other-two","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":" Centered (default) Lagged Semi-implicit Euler\nDashpot uses mathbf v_n+1 (implicit) mathbf v_n+12 mathbf v_n\nCost per step a linear solve one force evaluation one force evaluation\nStable step, one oscillator omegaDelta t le 2 omegaDelta t le 2left(sqrt1+zeta^2-zetaright) the same as lagged\nOrder, damped 2 1 1\nRestart stores mathbf r, mathbf v, contact history also mathbf a_n mathbf r, mathbf v, contact history","title":"Compared with the other two"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#Open-questions","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"How the linear solve is done (the method, its tolerance, any preconditioner) is not designed.\nWhether the solve cost outweighs the longer step on packings is not measured; a benchmark is planned.\nHow the run-time check estimates omega_textmax of a packing is not designed.","title":"Open questions"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#Theory","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nCentered velocity Verlet: the same steps with every property.\nIntegrator tests: the one-oscillator and network tests.\nTime step with damping: the stable-step derivation.","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#References","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"[Samiei2013] K. Samiei, B. Peters, M. Bolten, A. Frommer (2013). Assessment of the potentials of implicit integration method in discrete element modelling of granular matter. Computers & Chemical Engineering 49, 183–193. doi:10.1016/j.compchemeng.2012.10.009.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#integrator","page":"Overview","text":"Up: DampedDynamics()\n\nintegrator chooses how DampedDynamics() steps the particles forward in time when the dashpot force depends on velocity. The default is the centered velocity Verlet, the most accurate of the three, and its stable time step does not shrink as damping grows. The three choices differ in which velocity the dashpot uses at the end of a step, and that decides the cost per step, the stable step and what a restart file stores. FIRE's integrator is a separate setting of FIRE().","title":"integrator"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#The-call-with-the-default-written-out","page":"Overview","text":"pack(                                             # run the packing\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(                   # damped dynamics instead of the default FIRE\n        integrator = :centered_velocity_verlet,   # dashpot on the end-of-step velocity (default)\n    )                                             # end of DampedDynamics call\n)                                                 # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nalgorithm = DampedDynamics(...) — damped dynamics, Newton's equations with contact dashpots.\nintegrator = :centered_velocity_verlet (default) — the dashpot force uses the end-of-step velocity mathbf v_n+1.\nFor details, see :centered_velocity_verlet.","title":"The call with the default written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#Why-the-dashpot-needs-a-choice","page":"Overview","text":"With a dashpot the new acceleration depends on a velocity mathbf v^ast:\n\nmathbf a_n+1 =\nfrac\nmathbf F(mathbf r_n+1) -\nunderbrace\ngammamathbf v^ast\n_textdashpot\nm\n\nVelocity Verlet computes mathbf v_n+1 from mathbf a_n+1, so mathbf v_n+1 does not exist yet when mathbf a_n+1 is needed.\nThe three choices differ only in mathbf v^ast: the half-step velocity mathbf v_n+12 (lagged), the old velocity mathbf v_n (Euler), or mathbf v_n+1 solved for (centered).","title":"Why the dashpot needs a choice"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Dashpot force uses Cost per step Stable step, one damped oscillator Order, damped Restart stores \n:centered_velocity_verlet mathbf v_n+1 (implicit) a linear solve over the contacts omegaDelta t le 2 2 mathbf r, mathbf v, contact history default\n:lagged_velocity_verlet mathbf v_n+12 one force evaluation omegaDelta t le 2left(sqrt1+zeta^2-zetaright) 1 also the forces and torques of the last evaluation \n:semi_implicit_euler mathbf v_n one force evaluation the same as lagged 1 mathbf r, mathbf v, contact history ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#Why-the-default","page":"Overview","text":"Of the three, the centered form is the most accurate, and its stable step does not shrink with damping (SoftPack choice).\nIn a one-oscillator test it was second order at zeta = 001 to 098, while the other two were first order from zeta approx 03.\nIts cost is a linear solve every step, and [Samiei2013] find that implicit integration in DEM \"enables larger time steps\" but \"the overall speed up is overruled by higher computational costs of the implicit method\".\nSoftPack note: the default is to be checked against that cost on packings, and if the solve dominates, the default changes.\nWe did not find a paper that compares these three for relaxing jammed packings with contact dashpots (a few searches, 2026-11-24).","title":"Why the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#Restart-and-checks","page":"Overview","text":"The restart file records the integrator and refuses a restart with a different one.\nThe run-time check of timestep compares the time step with the stable step of the chosen integrator and warns when it is exceeded.","title":"Restart and checks"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#Related-keywords","page":"Overview","text":"damping: the dashpots whose force the integrator steps.\ntimestep: the step the stable-step check compares against.","title":"Related keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#Theory","page":"Overview","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nAlgorithms and combinations: the setting, the reason for the default and the integrator tests.\nTime step with damping: the stable-step derivation.\nRestart state: why the lagged form stores forces and torques.","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Samiei2013] K. Samiei, B. Peters, M. Bolten, A. Frommer (2013). Assessment of the potentials of implicit integration method in discrete element modelling of granular matter. Computers & Chemical Engineering 49, 183–193. doi:10.1016/j.compchemeng.2012.10.009.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ContactCount/#ContactCount()","page":"ContactCount()","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: ContactCount(cone_test = …); ContactCount (default); Cone test geometry.\n\nThis is the default for rattlers.","title":"ContactCount()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ContactCount/#Options","page":"ContactCount()","text":"cone_test","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RestartTest/#RestartTest()","page":"RestartTest()","text":"Up: convergence\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: RestartTest(); RestartTest.\n\nThe RelativeForce() test (default tolerance) ends every minimization. Once, on the final packing, all velocities are set to zero and the packing is accepted only if neither the kinetic energy nor the unbalanced forces grow back.","title":"RestartTest()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/#DampedDynamics()","page":"Overview","text":"Up: algorithm\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: algorithm = DampedDynamics(...); DampedDynamics.\n\nDamping, drag and the integrator are keywords of this constructor.","title":"DampedDynamics()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/#Keywords","page":"Overview","text":"damping\ndrag\nintegrator: the default is :centered_velocity_verlet.","title":"Keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/rattlers_file/#rattlers_file","page":"rattlers_file","text":"Up: write_packing()\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: writepacking(rattlersfile = …); Rattlers file (rattlers_file); A second file with every particle.","title":"rattlers_file"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/rattlers_file/#Choices","page":"rattlers_file","text":"Choice Default\nfalse default\ntrue ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"#SoftPack","page":"Home","text":"SoftPack is a Julia package for generating jammed packings of soft particles for granular and soft-matter simulations. The goal is one well-documented, reproducible place to make packings, so that packings used across studies are built the same way and described by the same metadata.\n\nStatus: design stage (no engine code yet). The pages describe the planned interface.","title":"SoftPack"},{"category":"section","location":"#Start-here","page":"Home","text":"SoftPack has two functions you call. Each page opens with every default written out and goes deeper only as far as you follow the links.\n\npack(): create a packing. It starts from the call with every default, then lists every option, then each option's choices, then each choice's own settings.\nwrite_packing(): save a packing to disk. It starts from the call with every default, then each option.","title":"Start here"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reference","page":"Home","text":"Naming rules: how keywords and types are named.\nUnits and length unit: the units every page uses.\nTheory pages: models, derivations and worked numbers behind the defaults.\nNotation: every symbol, its meaning and units.\nReferences: full citations for every source.","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"#Conventions","page":"Home","text":"Notation follows Notation: gamma is a dashpot coefficient (force per velocity), rates are r and a, and c is never damping.\nEquations are LaTeX: inline $...$, display $$...$$.\nSources are cited by key, for example [Somfai2005], with the full citation at the bottom of each page.\nQuotes are copied verbatim from the sources' full texts and keep the sources' own symbols.\nSoftPack choice marks a decision with no literature source, and SoftPack note marks our own derivation, estimate or unit conversion.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-these-pages-live","page":"Home","text":"The pages are Markdown files in docs/src/ of the SoftPack repository, and this site is built from them with Documenter.jl. The design document states each decision with its reasons and sources.","title":"Where these pages live"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/PackParams/#PackParams()","page":"PackParams()","text":"Up: pack()\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: 3. Re‑use a set of settings.\n\nReuse a set of settings: pack(params).","title":"PackParams()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/PackParams/#Options","page":"PackParams()","text":"any pack() keyword","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ViscousRate/#ViscousRate()","page":"ViscousRate()","text":"Up: damping\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: ViscousRate(normal, tangential); ViscousRate.","title":"ViscousRate()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ViscousRate/#Options","page":"ViscousRate()","text":"normal\ntangential","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/#protocol","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: pack(protocol = …); Isotropic compression; Compression of the default packing.\n\nHow the box is compressed to the target.\n\nDefault: IsotropicCompression(strain_step = 1e-2)","title":"protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nIsotropicCompression() default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/naming/#Naming-rules","page":"Naming rules","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: Interface and naming; design document §1.\n\nCamelCase types, snake_case keywords, object-then-property (old §1).","title":"Naming rules"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/KineticEnergy/#KineticEnergy()","page":"KineticEnergy()","text":"Up: convergence\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: KineticEnergy(tolerance = …); KineticEnergy.\n\nIt holds when the RelativeForce() test (default tolerance) holds and the mean kinetic energy per particle is below tolerance times P_texttargetd^D.","title":"KineticEnergy()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/KineticEnergy/#Options","page":"KineticEnergy()","text":"tolerance","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/steps_max/#steps_max","page":"steps_max","text":"Up: Available options\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: steps_max; Step cap (steps_max).\n\nDefault: 10^7","title":"steps_max"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/steps_max/#Choices","page":"steps_max","text":"Choice Default\n10^7 default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ContactDamping/#ContactDamping()","page":"ContactDamping()","text":"Up: damping\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: ContactDamping(normal = …); ContactDamping(tangential = …); ContactDamping(tangential_mass = …); ContactDamping(attractive = …); ContactDamping(coulomb_limit = …).\n\nThis is the default for damping.","title":"ContactDamping()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ContactDamping/#Options","page":"ContactDamping()","text":"normal\ntangential\ntangential_mass\nattractive\ncoulomb_limit","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/FIRE/#FIRE()","page":"FIRE()","text":"Up: algorithm\n\nwarning: Not written in the new format yet\nThis page is a placeholder in the new structure. The current text lives in the wiki: FIRE settings; Defaults and their reasons.\n\n11 parameters, each a section on this page.\n\nThis is the default for algorithm.","title":"FIRE()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/FIRE/#Options","page":"FIRE()","text":"timestep_max\ntimestep_min\ndelay\ntimestep_grow\ntimestep_shrink\nmixing_coefficient\nmixing_shrink\nuphill_max\ninertia_correction\ndelay_initial\nintegrator","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/FIRE/#Damping-and-drag","page":"FIRE()","text":"damping: contact dashpots for FIRE (default nothing).\ndrag: Stokes drag for FIRE (default nothing).","title":"Damping and drag"}]
